<p>You do great work. Your clients tell you so. They thank you at the door, they send you a nice message afterwards, they refer their friends. But when someone new searches for your type of business on Google, you are sitting there with 11 reviews while the competitor down the road has 190.</p>

<p>It is not because they are better. It is because they ask. Consistently, at the right time, through the right channel. Getting more Google reviews is not about luck or waiting for happy clients to remember. It is about having a system.</p>

<p>Here is how to build one that works.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Why Google reviews matter more than you think</h2>

<p>Google reviews are not just a vanity metric. They are a ranking signal. Google's local search algorithm looks at three things when deciding which businesses to show in the local pack: relevance, distance, and prominence. Prominence is heavily influenced by your review count, your average rating, and how recently those reviews were posted.</p>

<p>A business with 150 recent, positive reviews will outrank a business with 12 reviews from 2023. Even if the second business has a better website. Even if they have been around longer. Google uses reviews as a proxy for trust, and so do your potential customers.</p>

<p>The numbers back this up. Most people will not consider a business with fewer than 10 reviews, and the majority filter for 4 stars and above. If you are below those thresholds, you are invisible to a large portion of your market.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>When to ask (timing is everything)</h2>

<p>The single biggest factor in whether someone leaves a review is when you ask. The best moment is immediately after a positive experience, while the emotional peak is still fresh. Within 24 hours is the window. After that, the moment has passed and the request feels disconnected.</p>

<blockquote><p>You do not need more happy clients to get more reviews. You need to ask the happy ones at the right moment.</p></blockquote>

<p>Think about it from their side. Right after a great haircut, a successful training session, or a spotless house clean, they feel good. They are grateful. If a message lands on their phone while that feeling is still warm, they are far more likely to act on it. Wait a week and the motivation has evaporated.</p>

<h2>How to ask: SMS vs email</h2>

<p>You have two main channels for review requests, and they perform very differently.</p>

<p><strong><a href="/blog/sms-marketing-small-business">SMS</a> gets better results.</strong> Open rates for text messages sit above 90%, compared to roughly 20% for email. People read texts within minutes. The friction is low. A short message with a direct link to your Google review form is all it takes.</p>

<p><strong>Email works as a follow-up.</strong> If someone does not respond to your SMS within a few days, a gentle email reminder can pick up an extra 10-15% of responses. It also gives you room for a slightly longer message if needed.</p>

<p>The key for both: keep it short, keep it personal, and make it one tap to leave a review. Do not send people to your website and hope they find the review link. Send them directly to the Google review form.</p>

<h2>The exact ask (steal this)</h2>

<p>Here is what a good review request looks like:</p>

<p><em>"Hey Sarah, it was great seeing you today. If you have a minute, a quick Google review would mean the world to us. Here is the link: [one-tap link]"</em></p>

<p>That is it. No essay. No "it would be greatly appreciated if you could take a moment to share your feedback on our services." People are busy. Respect their time. Make it feel like a human sent it, not a marketing department.</p>

<p>Three things matter in the ask:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Use their name.</strong> Personalisation turns a broadcast into a conversation.</li>
  <li><strong>Reference the specific experience.</strong> "Great seeing you today" or "glad we could get your plumbing sorted" reminds them why they are happy.</li>
  <li><strong>One link, one action.</strong> The link should open the Google review form directly. No extra steps, no account creation, no confusion.</li>
</ul>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Automating the whole process</h2>

<p>The best review system is one you do not have to think about. Here is how to set it up:</p>

<p><strong>Trigger on completion.</strong> When an appointment is marked as complete in your <a href="/blog/booking-systems-that-convert">booking system</a>, the review request fires automatically. No manual sending, no forgetting on busy days.</p>

<p><strong>Follow up once.</strong> If they have not left a review within three days, send one follow-up message. Something like: "No pressure at all. Here is that link again if you get a chance." Then stop. Two messages is the limit. Three starts to feel like nagging.</p>

<p><strong>Track your numbers.</strong> Know your conversion rate. If you are sending 50 review requests a month and getting 5 reviews, that is a 10% conversion rate. That is normal. But with better timing and a better message, you can push that to 15-20%. Over a year, the difference between 60 reviews and 120 reviews is significant.</p>

<p>Tools like DUSA can handle this entire workflow. The review request triggers after each appointment, the follow-up sends itself, and you can see your conversion rate and review trends in one place.</p>

<h2>Responding to reviews (yes, all of them)</h2>

<p>Getting reviews is half the job. Responding to them is the other half, and it is a core part of your <a href="/blog/reputation-management">reputation management</a> strategy. Every review, positive or negative, deserves a reply.</p>

<p>For positive reviews, keep it simple. Thank them by name, mention something specific about their visit, and leave it at that. "Thanks so much, James. Glad we could get your kitchen looking sharp for the weekend." This shows future readers that there is a real person behind the business.</p>

<p>For negative reviews, here is the framework:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Respond within 24 hours.</strong> Speed signals that you care.</li>
  <li><strong>Acknowledge the issue.</strong> Do not get defensive. Even if you disagree, their experience was their experience.</li>
  <li><strong>Take it offline.</strong> "I would love to sort this out. Could you give us a call at [number] so we can make it right?"</li>
  <li><strong>Stay professional.</strong> Every response is a public performance. Other potential customers are reading it and deciding whether you are the kind of business they want to deal with.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote><p>A thoughtful response to a negative review builds more trust than a dozen five-star ratings with no replies.</p></blockquote>

<p>People understand that no business is perfect. What they want to see is that you care enough to respond and fix the problem. That tells them more about your business than any star rating could.</p>

